use {{copied_namespace}}\Kernel\Abstracts\UpdateHandler;

class {{class_name}} extends UpdateHandler {

	// Reach any declared module with `$this->with( Migrations::class )`.

	/**
	 * Bring this site up to date with the code that is now running.
	 *
	 * WordPress has no update hook -- `activate_{plugin}` does not fire on an
	 * update, and `upgrader_process_complete` runs while the OLD code is still in
	 * memory, and never at all for an FTP upload. So this compares your plugin's
	 * `Version:` header against the version recorded here last time. They differ
	 * exactly when new code is running, however it arrived.
	 *
	 * Runs on `admin_init`, so no front-end visitor carries the work. Override
	 * get_hook() to move it.
	 *
	 * $previous is null when this site has no recorded version -- a fresh
	 * install, or one that predates this handler. You cannot tell those apart, so
	 * do the work that brings a site up to date from anywhere: run_pending() is
	 * already exactly that, taking the baseline on a new site and the pending
	 * migrations on an old one.
	 *
	 * TRUE IS THE ONLY THING THAT MEANS DONE. Return false or a WP_Error, or
	 * throw, and the version is not recorded -- so this is retried -- and the
	 * message is shown as an admin notice. Nothing propagates: an update that
	 * fataled on every admin request would lock you out of the screen you would
	 * fix it from.
	 *
	 * @param string|null $previous The version recorded last time, or null when there is none.
	 * @param string      $current  The version running now.
	 * @return bool|\WP_Error True when the work is done; false or a WP_Error to be retried.
	 */
	public function update( ?string $previous, string $current ): bool|\WP_Error {
		// $this->with( Migrations::class )->run_pending();

		// Work for sites coming from before a particular release. $previous is
		// null on a site that has never recorded one, so check for it first.
